# m2asc backend style

Conventions for `{Api}` (ASP.NET). Keep bullets short; expand only when a pattern is repeated.

## DI registration

- File/class: `DependencyInjection` (not `*ServiceCollectionExtensions`)
- Methods: `Add*Services(this IServiceCollection)`
- Location: beside the feature (e.g. `Conversion/DependencyInjection.cs`)

## CancellationToken

- Parameter name: `ct` (not `cancellationToken`)
- No default value (`= default` / `= default(CancellationToken)` forbidden)

## Interface + implementation

- One file per service pair: interface first, concrete type immediately after
- File name matches the implementation (e.g. `PixelCharMapper.cs` contains `IPixelCharMapper` then `PixelCharMapper`)
- Do not split into `IFoo.cs` + `Foo.cs`
- Co-locate small result/DTO types used only by that service in the same file (e.g. `VideoAsciiResult` with `IVideoAsciiConverter` / `VideoAsciiConverter`)
- Split those types out only when shared across features or they grow real behavior

## Endpoint DTOs + validators

- Request/response types live under `Endpoints/Dtos/`
- FluentValidation validators live in the **same file** as their DTO (DTO first, validator immediately after); file name matches the DTO (e.g. `ConvertFormRequest.cs` contains `ConvertFormRequest` then `ConvertFormRequestValidator`)
- Do not put validators in a separate file or under `Infrastructure/`
- Validation limits / allowed-value sets belong on the validator, not on the DTO
- Endpoint framework glue (filters, `AddAutoValidation`, etc.) lives under `Endpoints/Infrastructure/`

## Constructor injection

- Inject dependencies via primary constructors (no separate ctor body for DI wiring)

## Async naming

- Do not postfix method names with `Async` (e.g. prefer `Convert` over `ConvertAsync`)
- Modern .NET APIs are async by default here; the suffix adds noise without clarity
